Critical thinking is domain-transferable: skills learned analysing historical arguments transfer to evaluating medical claims, financial decisions, and news stories.
The ability to identify logical fallacies — ad hominem, false dichotomy, appeal to authority — directly improves resistance to persuasion techniques used in advertising and political messaging.
Employers consistently rate 'critical thinking' as the most important and most difficult to find skill in graduates — schools that teach it produce more employable students.
